# Text extensions are normalised to LF on checkout. The vendored tree is exempt,
# and that rule comes LAST because git resolves attribute conflicts by taking
# the pattern that appears later in this file -- measured 2026-11-12: with the
# vendor rule at the top, every `.py` under it still resolved to `text: set`,
# so the exemption applied to the one extension-less file (`LICENSE`) and to
# nothing else. It looked like it worked.
#
# Why the exemption exists: `vendor/tars/MANIFEST.sha256` records the digest of
# each file as it exists upstream, which is LF. If a checkout rewrites those
# bytes the vendor check reports thirty-two mismatches that are all the same
# mismatch, and the real question (was this copy edited?) is buried.
# Measured 2026-11-03: 32 of 32 files carried CRLF, 7291 line endings in all;
# after normalising, 32 of 32 matched the manifest and nothing else changed.
#
# Measured 2026-11-11, the day before the first commit: 191 files in the tree,
# 85 of them CRLF and 13 starting with a BOM, while the rules below covered
# three extensions. Every text extension is listed now, and the working tree
# itself was normalised to LF in the same pass -- a .gitattributes that only
# fixes checkouts leaves the source it ships from inconsistent. A blanket
# `* text=auto eol=lf` would do this in one line, but then the icon and the
# manifest depend on git guessing which files are binary; naming the
# extensions keeps that a decision.
#
# `/LICENSE` is anchored so it does not also match `vendor/tars/LICENSE`.

*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.sha256 text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
*.gitignore text eol=lf
*.gitattributes text eol=lf
*.patch text eol=lf
/LICENSE text eol=lf

# `**/` is required: a pattern containing a slash is anchored to the repository
# root, so plain `vendor/tars/**` would match nothing here.
**/vendor/tars/** -text
